This task asks us to find the minimum value of a list that has been rotated, meaning it is not sorted from min to max.
However, we can stil use binary search, as the list is still in an increasing order except for a single break, which is where the list was rotated to.
As with any binary search, we start by defining our left and right variables.
Left is zero, and right is the length of nums minus one.
Knowing that the list is in order, we need to find the location of the rotation, as that's where the maximum and minimum values are.
We start with a while loop that ensures the left index is never equal to or greater than the right index, as that's how we'll know that we've seen every item in the list.
We define our mid variable, which is the average of left and right rounded down using floor division to ensure an integer value.
Our first conditional statement checks to see if the number in the middle is greater than the number on the right.
If it is, we bring our left boundary up to one past middle, as we now know the minimum is somewhere between the middle and the right.
if it isn't, we bring our right boundary down by setting it equal to middle.
The key here is that we don't set the right boundary to middle - 1, just middle, because we want to eventually return left.
Since mid could be the minimum in the second scenario, we want to ensure that we are still accounting for it's value.
Running through this while loop will eventually ensure that the value of left will correspond to the index of the minimum value of the list.
We finish by returning the number at index left within the list nums.
